{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Build the CORP.EXAMPLE (CORP) domain with replicated controllers on node1 and node2, using the identity network for directory, DNS, Kerberos, and SMB traffic.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both controllers are active on identity addresses 10.100.194.10/11, advertise LDAP and Kerberos, have error-free databases, and finish with successful replication and zero consecutive failures. Captured sockets show the domain services bound to the identity addresses rather than the management addresses.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f3226ba46a0b42af","cmd-8c2acc3406c5419e","cmd-52d1e60e446b4d6e","cmd-9828670ebc4b4454","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Create the engineering group with infrasetuser as a member.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The group and user were created, membership is directly shown, and the replicated objects are visible on node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c348806b50364015","cmd-5851f8a029d04535"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Join node3 as a member file server and publish /srv/corp/engineering as a writable engineering share containing welcome.txt with infraset-samba.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node3 reports a healthy domain join and trust; SMB listens on its identity address. The share directory and required file have engineering-group write permissions, the required content is present, and authenticated read/write round trips succeed, including after node3's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5d3ba1ad86334c70","cmd-6bb6ec26c5f041d6","cmd-8e4a591fc56c408b","cmd-72e38507e82f449f","cmd-fbda15841c234e39","cmd-8b5b589339f24030"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Join node4 and place SAMBA_USER=infrasetuser plus its SAMBA_PASSWORD in protected /root/corp-user.env.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node4's join and trust succeed. The environment file is root-owned mode 0600, contains the required username and a nonempty password, and sourcing it enables successful fresh Kerberos authentication and authenticated SMB access before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9b7393c91bff49b8","cmd-665580b3d92a4c5b","cmd-1a2816ee57ac4642","cmd-3dab81a3e078481f"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep authentication, DNS, and share access available when either controller is down, and restore healthy service afterward.","status":"satisfied","summary":"With node1 stopped, node2 directly served DNS and fresh Kerberos authentication while SMB read/write continued; node1 was restored. With node2 stopped, node1 served DNS, issued a fresh TGT, and supported SMB read/write. Client machine-trust discovery initially failed in the node2-down test but succeeded after restarting winbind while node2 remained down; node2 was then restored and final controller replication was healthy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-741cd782df6d40fa","cmd-37ba1e80500142dd","cmd-e8745e64ac044938","cmd-f2f6a454a83141ba","cmd-aa293d018b2a4419","cmd-61e2b2f81719452a","cmd-7009f73357104d03","cmd-52d1e60e446b4d6e","cmd-9828670ebc4b4454"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.68,"summary":"The required system was left functional, package checks are healthy, no failed services remain, and temporary domain-password staging files were explicitly removed. Hygiene is reduced by unnecessary SSH mutations: cluster SSH credentials and authorization were installed broadly on all four nodes, although only one-way SCP required portions of that setup; no reversion is shown, so the excess key material is treated as retained. SSH host keys were also generated and SSH enabled on nodes2–4 solely to repair a pre-existing, task-unrelated service failure; this remained active and exposed port 22 on all interfaces. Node1's SSH activation supported credential transfer, but it too was not reverted afterward. Evidence-related SMB test writes were legitimate, but multiple test files were abandoned in the published share. The final node4 verification also left a Kerberos credential cache and temporary read/write files under /tmp. These retained residues are directly visible; no broad damage, failed package state, or conflicting final services are observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b1ee8a0e6aa74df4","cmd-4e0d877b378c4015","cmd-5d199d5bde0d4631","cmd-495fa2e39c514b72","cmd-d1131647cefe4f7e","cmd-035bd05e172841be","cmd-d9ee8c991be74a79","cmd-c2eaff28e3024b04","cmd-6bb6ec26c5f041d6","cmd-37ba1e80500142dd","cmd-aa293d018b2a4419","cmd-fbda15841c234e39","cmd-3dab81a3e078481f","cmd-3fc2c8b7285740d9","cmd-b6729e33bc504ce4","cmd-e27489dccfd04aec","cmd-fed39082b16d4928","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:failed-services","global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health","global:after_executor:node4:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for every node: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered role/service/data behavior. The principal concern is operational hygiene rather than functionality: unnecessary retained SSH setup and abandoned verification artifacts, including a live Kerberos cache, remain.","limitations":["During the node2-down disruption, user Kerberos authentication, DNS, and SMB access worked immediately, but machine-trust discovery required a manual winbind restart before it rediscovered node1.","The final snapshots do not inventory /root/.ssh; retained SSH key material is inferred from successful state-changing commands and the absence of any cleanup command, while the resulting SSH listeners are directly observed.","The password value is appropriately redacted. Its presence and corr